n{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4-\\lambda  \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(20-4\\lambda -5\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-16)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4-4) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186184878 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186184878 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186184878 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186184878 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186184880 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values\n- **II.** Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values.\n- **III.** The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ has two non-negative singular values." 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186184880 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values\n- **II.** Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values.\n- **III.** The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has two non-negative singular values." 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186185315 1064 "A matrix is said to be **nilpotent** if some positive power of the matrix is the zero matrix. For example, the matrix\n\n$$B = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis nilpotent because its second power is the zero matrix:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =O\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo state the definition more precisely: a matrix with the property $B^k=O$ for a number $k>0$ is known as a **nilpotent** matrix, and the lowest number $k$ such that $B^k=O$ is called the **index.**\n\nTherefore, our matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186185315 1064 "A matrix is said to be **nilpotent** if some positive power of the matrix is the zero matrix. For example, the matrix\n\n$$B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nis nilpotent because its second power is the zero matrix:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =O\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo state the definition more precisely: a matrix with the property $B^k=O$ for a number $k>0$ is known as a **nilpotent** matrix, and the lowest number $k$ such that $B^k=O$ is called the **index.**\n\nTherefore, our matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186185316 1064 "A matrix is said to be **idempotent**, when multiplied by itself, yields itself.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$B = [\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis idempotent because $B^2 = B$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot (-6)+(-6)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =B\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186185316 1064 "A matrix is said to be **idempotent**, when multiplied by itself, yields itself.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nis idempotent because $B^2 = B$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot (-6)+(-6)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =B\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186185322 1064 "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positive semi-definite matrix, the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$ must be always non-negative real numbers.\n\nLet's compute the singular values of the matrix $A,$ given by \n\n![](images/a9/a9edbe1fee59758c9557d95ea8f62468f9f3cb98449c437a274bdbf5c30f7dc6.svg)\n\nNow, since\n\n- $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix, and\n\n- $A^T$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\nthen $A^T \\! A$  must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 3}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 3} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nLet's now compute our singular values. First, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When listing the singular values of a matrix, we usually write them in descending order.\n\n- If $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, then $A^T A$ is a $n\\times n$ positive semi-definite matrix. This means that $A$ has *at most* $n$ distinct singular values." 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186185322 1064 "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positi