$r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/de/dec5e32e503bf178832f6cf08b51767d2f76fb308e9a9d07c4e805389507bbfd.png)" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186221151 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/de/dec5e32e503bf178832f6cf08b51767d2f76fb308e9a9d07c4e805389507bbfd.png)" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186221152 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(6t)={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =8{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1+2t), \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(-2t), \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(2t), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/70/70c4e625ac52b04e9a1fb258470138050a51cdacaced8714cd2a313782055c3f.png)" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186221152 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(6t)={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =8{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1+2t), \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(-2t), \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(2t), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/70/70c4e625ac52b04e9a1fb258470138050a51cdacaced8714cd2a313782055c3f.png)" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186221170 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/b0/b0b5abdfe48d45a352b101e134ebb06165824385c1162a705e539f911af9f267.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/96/962887c2e859c2afadfc122bd5ecc22fe28eb354b348c5153c16577de5fe37d8.svg) are equilibrium solutions of the system sinc