/96/962887c2e859c2afadfc122bd5ecc22fe28eb354b348c5153c16577de5fe37d8.svg) are equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfc7b00f7f63aec120179da3f89f72f0aa53f13d299c95ed8f53e6238d329e51.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n![](images/9b/9bbb4470407c8498cf3270e247e7b9a9d214bd1ff1b3d85a75d17189bee26ea9.svg)\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t+2 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/55/55dfdfce480deba74fe46c1634e4c7ffb711fbd617525cdb5387bdabfc83ae75.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t-2 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/2f/2fb2297c741ea20ca729dcacb9934d7f8d1cca58af93c2b75db177890934b2b6.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e7970ba2d6bbe4745ee498cdbc72f2b7d8689219291847644bd6483696cc456.png)" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186221188 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/cd/cd022d759a5ce5a8521687d42eaf38053339212301a6b3559ad4bee935f69317.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f5/f51930ce883e8e2f4865d846b38c475e68875565593ad58044125e81f0443718.svg)\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/53/53cec1a04bca84fba279c48cf5a531674040c07e2a1dac12748107b91809ff72.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/05/05e9d31a8413096e60f8d30b8206052375dc7c9df5895568a33b475fca22fc72.svg)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} 