3a.svg)\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order." 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186221192 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t),$ denoted $\\Phi_0(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0)  = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((7\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(7\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(7\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+7\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/74/744b1de9acba2740bac7ebc54d79fb7ae82e98cb0576492f4d9503bb0b2a71a8.svg)" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186221192 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t),$ denoted $\\Phi_0(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0)  = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & -7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((7\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(7\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(7\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+7\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/74/744b1de9acba2740bac7ebc54d79fb7ae82e98cb0576492f4d9503bb0b2a71a8.svg)" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186221194 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big) = A \\, e^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & t{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & (t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186221194 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big) = A \\, e^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & t{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & (t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186221922 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." 1319