_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(7{e}^{-4t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{-4t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+7\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (-2{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot (-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot (-2{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-20{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,c}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(7{e}^{-4t})\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,c}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186221923 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 &