rst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 1 \\\\ -2(1) & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda \\cdot (-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186221971 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\