b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{b}{|b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233367 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{b}{|b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233368 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction." 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233368 1071 "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction." 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233368 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+b & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+5 \\\\ 4+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2a+b| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(2a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233368 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+b & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+5 \\\\ 4+8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2a+b| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(2a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233379 1071 "If\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233379 1071 "If\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233380 1071 "Calculate the minor of the entry ${p}_{32}$ in the matrix\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233380 1071 "Calculate the minor of the entry ${p}_{32}$ in the matrix\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233380 1074 "The minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ (third row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}8 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 3 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8\\cdot 6-4\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =48+20 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233380 1074 "The minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ (third row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 3 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 6-4\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =48+20 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233433 1071 "Find $K+L-(K+J)$, if\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233433 1071 "Find $K+L-(K+J)$, if\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,K=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,L=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233433 1074 "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K+L-(K+J) & =K+L-K-J \\\\  & =\\cancel{K}+L-\\cancel{K}-J \\\\  & =L-J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L-J & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-5 & 6-2 \\\\ 6-2 & 6-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233433 1074 "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K+L-(K+J) & =K+L-K-J \\\\  & =\\cancel{K}+L-\\cancel{K}-J \\\\  & =L-J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L-J & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6-5 & 6-2 \\\\ 6-2 & 6-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233605 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ns=i-6j=⟨1,-6,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTo compute the sum, we just add each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r+s & =⟨0,-2,4⟩+⟨1,-6,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,-2-6,4+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-8,4⟩ \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233605 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ns=i-6j=⟨1,-6,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTo compute the sum, we just add each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r+s & =⟨0,-2,4⟩+⟨1,-6,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,-2-6,4+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-8,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233735 1071 "Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $v.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233735 1071 "Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $v.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233735 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,v\\,| & =\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $v$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233735 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,v\\,| & =\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $v$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233736 1071 "Given that $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $w.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233736 1071 "Given that $w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $w.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233736 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,w\\,| & =\\sqrt{{w}_{x}^{2}+{w}_{y}^{2}+{w}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $w$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233736 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,w\\,| & =\\sqrt{{w}_{x}^{2}+{w}_{y}^{2}+{w}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $w$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186233827 1074 "Remember that in general, if a point $B$ has coordinates $({b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=⟨{b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}⟩=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{x} \\\\ {b}_{y} \\\\ {b}_{z}\\end{aligned}]={b}_{x}\\,i+{b}_{y}\\,j+{b}_{z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =-7i+2j+5k \\\\  & =-7\\cdot i+2\\cdot j+5\\cdot k \\\\  & =⟨-7,2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186233827 1074 "Remember that in general, if a point $B$ has coordinates $({b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=⟨{b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}⟩=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{x} \\\\ {b}_{y} \\\\ {b}_{z}\\end{bmatrix}={b}_{x}\\,i+{b}_{y}\\,j+{b}_{z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =-7i+2j+5k \\\\  & =-7\\cdot i+2\\cdot j+5\\cdot k \\\\  & =⟨-7,2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186234271 1071 "Let $n=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $n$ and points the same direction." 13194139535474 17592186045443 false] [17592186234271 1071 "Let $n=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $n$ and points the same direction." 13194139535474 17592186045443 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
